  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to perform geometry-inspired optimization by carefully seeding the initial function with a symmetric, ripple-like pattern and then using directional gradients and repair mechanisms to minimize the maximum overlap. Unlike previous algorithms relying on soft-max surrogates and jitter-based perturbations, this approach combines a structured geometric initialization with local gradient information and periodic repairs. The key limitations addressed are:\n1. Improved identification of worst-case shifts through targeted directional optimization\n2. More efficient use of budget via structured seeding and gradient-free upgrades\n3. Better handling of boundary conditions through explicit interval balance metrics\n4. Enhanced robustness through hybrid repair methods\n\nMathematical specification\nThe objective is C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx, with:\n0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n\u222bh(x)dx = 1\nh is represented as n-point samples over [0,2] with dx=2/n\n\nThe initializer uses:\nh_initial[i] = (1 - 2|phase - 0.5| + noise_term) for i=0..n-1, where:\nphase = (i * 2/n) % 1\n\nThen phases that implement first-order derivatives:\n_exact_grad(h): Computes gradient using:\n  dx = [\u03b4_C_\u03b4h_i for all i] \n  \u03b4_C_\u03b4h_i = \u2211_k dx * [abspeak_shift[k] when contribution from h_i affects C]\n\nConstraints are handled by:\n1. Repair function that maintains sum=1 while projecting out-of-bounds\n2. L1 projection onto [0,1] space\n3. Non-local repair when sum diverges\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   if initial_h_values exists:\n      n0 = len(initial_h_values)\n      h = _repair(np.array(initial_h_values).copy(), n0)\n   else:\n      n0 = 560\n      h = geometric_initial(n0)\n\n2. Phase 1: Directional seeding (budget=300s)\n   - Detect worst shift k_max at initialization\n   - Augment h by adding 0.01-amplitude sine ripples at base frequency 2/period\n   - Compute new gradient using finite differences\n\n3. Phase 2: Geometry-driven optimization (budget=400s)\n   - Target worst shifts (10-key) from initial C5\n   - Project gradient onto a spatial grid using:\n        \u0398 = [all i such that shift k_max affects integral]\n        g_i = \u2211_{s in \u0398} \u03b4h_i contribution\n   - Clip gradient components with [0,0.05] amplitude\n\n4. Phase 3: Periodic repair (budget=200s)\n   - Trim/clone endpoints to balance integral\n   - Iterative global repair:\n        repeat until sum(h)=target or max_iter:\n        h_new = _exact_proj(h, target)\n        h_new = np.clip(h_new,0,1)\n\n5. Phase 4: Final polish (budget=200s)\n   - Down-frequency projection to primary frequencies\n   - Gradient descent with L2 penalty to smooth edges using:\n        C5_new = _eval_c5_shifts(h+\u03b5*g)\n\nSearch choices\nPrimary settings:\n  n = 560 (adjust for other scales)\n  seed: based on seed+offset\n  tolerance 0.001 for gradient clipping\n  target_sum = n/2\n  max_iter_per_phase: 400\n\nSafety mechanisms:\n  - Detect sign flips in gradient and revert\n  - Force repair when sum exceeds 10% tolerance\n  - Use L2-norm for gradient iteration leveling\n\nComputebudget and robustness\nMain cost: Phases 3 and 4\nSubsidiary (40-60s):\n  - Gradient evaluation (complex dependency tracing)\n  - Global repair iteration (project+clip)\n\nTo estimate worst-case:\n  T = 1100s / 5 = 220s per phase\n\nValidation requirements:\n1. At least one valid solution per search\n2. sum(h) closest to n/2\n3. _eval_c5 returns finite number\n4. Gradient is within [0,0.05] bounds after clipping\n\nFallback: Geometric seed when initial failure occurs",
